Q: How can I allow &lt;input type=&quot;file&quot;&gt; to accept only image files? A: <p>This can be achieved by</p>
<pre><code>&lt;input type=&quot;file&quot; accept=&quot;image/*&quot; /&gt; 
</code></pre>
<p>But this is not a good way. you have to code on the server side to check if the file is an image or not.</p>
<p>Check if image file is an actual image or fake image:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>if (isset($_POST[&quot;submit&quot;])) {
    $check = getimagesize($_FILES[&quot;fileToUpload&quot;][&quot;tmp_name&quot;]);
    if($check !== false) {
        echo &quot;File is an image - &quot; . $check[&quot;mime&quot;] . &quot;.&quot;;
        $uploadOk = 1;
    }
    else {
        echo &quot;File is not an image.&quot;;
        $uploadOk = 0;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>For more reference, see here:<br />
<a href="http://www.w3schools.com/tags/att_input_accept.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">http://www.w3schools.com/tags/att_input_accept.asp</a><br />
<a href="http://www.w3schools.com/php/php_file_upload.asp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">http://www.w3schools.com/php/php_file_upload.asp</a></p>

Q: How to add doc comments for variables returned from a function call? A: <p>You can document the inner function that is returned.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function useFrobnaticator() {
  /**
   * Frobnicate a string value.
   * 
   * @param stringToFrobnicate (string) - A string value to frobnicate
   */
  return (stringToFrobnicate: string) =&gt; {
    console.log(
      stringToFrobnicate.length &gt; 20 ? &quot;(too long)&quot; : stringToFrobnicate
    );
  };
}
</code></pre>
<p>I don't have PyCharm for testing but this appears to work for VSCode and Intellisense-based IDEs. PyCharm supports <a href="https://www.jetbrains.com/help/pycharm/creating-jsdoc-comments.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">JSDoc</a> comments, and it appears that TSDoc aims for JSDoc compatibility, so I think the above will work for your purpose.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/mL6PvZAD.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/mL6PvZAD.png" alt="screenshot of documentation" /></a></p>

Q: cd to directory starting with &#39;-&#39; dash A: <p>As a rule of thumb, if your filenames have spaces or special chars like <code>$</code> put them in single or double quotes. As another rule of thumb, if one of your arguments starts with a <code>-</code> and your command is interpreting it as an option instead of a filename (an option like the <code>-n</code> in in <code>echo -n myfile</code>) then you need to put a <code>--</code> as an argument to your command:</p>
<pre><code>cd -- &quot;- folder1 -&quot;
</code></pre>

Q: Extract directory structure from for loop search A: <pre><code>@ECHO OFF
SETLOCAL ENABLEDELAYEDEXPANSION 
set &quot;SOURCE_DIR=U:\Level1\Level2&quot;
set &quot;OUTPUT_DIR=U:\ExtractedData&quot;

for /r %SOURCE_DIR% %%b in (*.tar.gz, *.tgz) do (
 SET &quot;levels=%%~dpb&quot;
 SET &quot;levels=!levels:%SOURCE_DIR%\=!&quot;
 IF /i &quot;%%~xb&quot;==&quot;.tgz&quot; (
  mkdir %OUTPUT_DIR%\!levels!%%~nb
  ECHO tar -xzvkf &quot;%%b&quot; -C %OUTPUT_DIR%\!levels!%%~nb
 ) ELSE (
  FOR %%B IN (%%~nb) do (
   mkdir %OUTPUT_DIR%\!levels!%%~nB
   ECHO tar -xzvkf &quot;%%b&quot; -C %OUTPUT_DIR%\!levels!%%~nB
  )
 )
)
GOTO :EOF

</code></pre>
<p>I don't have any <code>.tar.gz, .tgz</code> on my machine (that I know anything about) so the above is tested only in theory, hence the <code>tar</code> commands are <code>echo</code>ed - after verification, remove the <code>echo</code> keyword to activate the extraction.</p>
<p>I've changed to directory names to suit my system.</p>
<p>Your original code won't work as posted since the spaces in to two <code>set</code> commands would set variables whose name contained the space.</p>
<p>I don't like using <code>F</code> as a metavariable since it can be confused with a modifier, hence I changed to <code>B/b</code>.</p>
<p>I also like neither terminal backslashes nor quotes in values assigned as I find manipulating the strings &quot;raw&quot; and adding these characters &quot;manually&quot; makes the coding easier. Having said that, I turned a blind eye to my rule in this case where <code>!levels!</code> contains a terminal backslash, rather than using <code>!levels:~0,-1!\</code></p>
